Starting with rows `(1, 100)` and `(2, 50)` in `acct(id PRIMARY KEY, bal)`, what does the final query return?

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE acct SET bal = 0 WHERE id = 2;
SAVEPOINT s1;
INSERT INTO acct VALUES (1, 5);   -- ERROR: duplicate key
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT s1;
COMMIT;
SELECT * FROM acct ORDER BY id;
```

Two app servers run this code at the same moment for the same product, starting at `stock = 10`. Both run in READ COMMITTED. What is the most likely final stock, and what is the fix?

```sql
-- app code, per request
BEGIN;
SELECT stock FROM products WHERE id = 7;      -- app reads 10
-- app computes 10 - 1 = 9
UPDATE products SET stock = 9 WHERE id = 7;
COMMIT;
```

What happens when the third statement runs?

```sql
BEGIN;
SELECT count(*) FROM orders;
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
```

`acct` id 1 has `bal = 100`. Session A uses READ COMMITTED. What does A's second SELECT return?

```sql
-- Session A                         -- Session B
BEGIN;
SELECT bal FROM acct WHERE id = 1;    -- 100
                                      UPDATE acct SET bal = 80 WHERE id = 1;
                                      -- (autocommit: committed)
SELECT bal FROM acct WHERE id = 1;    -- ?
COMMIT;
```

`acct` id 1 has `bal = 100`. What does Session A's SELECT return?

```sql
-- Session A                                -- Session B
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
                                             UPDATE acct SET bal = 80 WHERE id = 1;
                                             -- (autocommit: committed)
SELECT bal FROM acct WHERE id = 1;           -- ?
COMMIT;
```

`acct` id 1 has `bal = 100`. What happens at A's UPDATE?

```sql
-- Session A                                -- Session B
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
SELECT bal FROM acct WHERE id = 1;  -- 100
                                             UPDATE acct SET bal = 80 WHERE id = 1;
                                             -- (autocommit: committed)
UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - 10 WHERE id = 1;
```

`acct` id 1 has `bal = 100`. Both sessions use READ COMMITTED. What is the final balance?

```sql
-- Session A                                -- Session B
BEGIN;
UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - 30 WHERE id = 1;
                                             BEGIN;
                                             UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - 50 WHERE id = 1;
                                             -- (waits)
COMMIT;
                                             -- (proceeds)
                                             COMMIT;
```

`acct` id 1 has `bal = 100`. Both sessions use READ COMMITTED. How many rows does B's UPDATE change?

```sql
-- Session A                                -- Session B
BEGIN;
UPDATE acct SET bal = 40 WHERE id = 1;
                                             UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - 50
                                              WHERE id = 1 AND bal >= 50;
                                             -- (waits)
COMMIT;
```

A rule says the app must always keep at least one admin. Two admins, Ana and Bo, exist. Both sessions run in REPEATABLE READ at the same time. What is the outcome?

```sql
-- Session A (demotes Ana)              -- Session B (demotes Bo)
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;  BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
SELECT count(*) FROM users             SELECT count(*) FROM users
 WHERE role = 'admin';  -- 2             WHERE role = 'admin';  -- 2
UPDATE users SET role = 'user'         UPDATE users SET role = 'user'
 WHERE name = 'Ana';                     WHERE name = 'Bo';
COMMIT;                                COMMIT;
```

Two transfers run at the same time in READ COMMITTED. What happens?

```sql
-- Session A                                -- Session B
BEGIN;                                       BEGIN;
UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - 10 WHERE id = 1;
                                             UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - 10 WHERE id = 2;
UPDATE acct SET bal = bal + 10 WHERE id = 2;
                                             UPDATE acct SET bal = bal + 10 WHERE id = 1;
```

What does the final SELECT return? `acct` id 1 starts at `bal = 100`.

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE acct SET bal = 1 WHERE id = 1;
SAVEPOINT s;
UPDATE acct SET bal = 2 WHERE id = 1;
SAVEPOINT s;
UPDATE acct SET bal = 3 WHERE id = 1;
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT s;
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT s;
SELECT bal FROM acct WHERE id = 1;
```

In one session, what do these two queries return?

```sql
SELECT pg_try_advisory_lock(42) AS a, pg_try_advisory_lock(42) AS b;
SELECT pg_advisory_unlock(42) AS c, pg_advisory_unlock(42) AS d, pg_advisory_unlock(42) AS e;
```

`acct` id 1 has `bal = 100`. Both sessions use READ COMMITTED. What is the final balance? Type a number.

```sql
-- Session A                                -- Session B
BEGIN;
UPDATE acct SET bal = bal * 2 WHERE id = 1;
                                             BEGIN;
                                             UPDATE acct SET bal = bal + 10 WHERE id = 1;
                                             -- (waits)
COMMIT;
                                             COMMIT;
```

`acct` id 1 has `bal = 100`. What happens at A's locking read?

```sql
-- Session A                                -- Session B
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
SELECT count(*) FROM acct;  -- takes snapshot
                                             UPDATE acct SET bal = 0 WHERE id = 1;
                                             -- (autocommit: committed)
SELECT bal FROM acct WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE;
```
